 Architecture

 Step 1 — Ingestion Tab (app.py "Ingest" tab, logic in ingest_agent.py)

 Trigger: two inputs in the UI, then a button.

 - Folder path — st.text_input("Project folder path"), prefilled from .last_ingest.json if present. (Streamlit has no native OS folder-browser dialog for a server-side path, so a text input with the remembered last value is the pragmatic choice — worth stating plainly in the interview rather than over-building a custom file-browser widget.)
 - Customer name — st.text_input("Customer name"), prefilled the same way. Always typed by the user, never inferred from the folder/project name — a project folder is normally named after the project, not the customer, and guessing would risk silently mis-scoping data between customers, which is the one thing this tool depends on for trust.
 - "Run Ingestion Agent" button — on click: save both inputs to .last_ingest.json, then call ingest_agent.run_ingestion(customer_name, folder_path) synchronously (with a st.spinner), then show a success summary (files read, chunks saved) or an error.
 - The customer name is slugified into the context_tag used for retrieval scoping (e.g. "Acme Retail" → acme-retail). This is the single mechanism that isolates one customer's data from another's.
 - Deterministic file reading: walks the given folder (project files + meeting summary files, both just treated as files — no special-casing between the two categories), reads each .txt/.md/.pdf, reusing extract_content_from_bytes() from vector_store.py so there's exactly one place in the codebase that knows how to parse a file. Plain Python, not an agent-invoked tool — deterministic and easy to debug.
 - Agent-driven web search: the LLM agent's job is narrow — given the customer name and a skim of the local files, formulate a good web search query and run it (DuckDuckGoSearchRun, same as References/AI-Agent/tools.py), optionally producing a short synthesized "customer briefing" paragraph. This is the one place create_tool_calling_agent + AgentExecutor genuinely earns its keep — everything else is plain code.
 - Indexing: all content (each local file + the web search result/synthesis) becomes a Document(page_content=..., metadata={"source": ..., "context_tag": ...}), chunked via create_database.py's split_text() (reuse chunk_size=300/overlap=100 as-is), saved via save_to_pgvector(chunks, pre_delete_collection=False) — never reset the collection, since multiple customers share one pgvector collection distinguished only by context_tag.
 - Direct Python function calls, not HTTP to /index. The ingestion script imports and calls create_database.py/vector_store.py functions directly in-process. Justification: agent and RAG store are the same codebase; an HTTP hop to your own FastAPI app adds serialization + a "is the server up?" failure mode for no decoupling benefit. api.py's /index endpoint still exists and works if ever needed as a shared service boundary — just unused here by choice.
 - No LLM-based relevance filtering at ingestion. Everything found gets indexed; relevance is computed once, at query time, via vector similarity + the existing min_relevance threshold. Avoids a second "judgment" layer that would be hard to explain and easy to get wrong.                                                                                                                                                                         
 Step 2 — Ask Tab (app.py "Ask" tab)                                                       
 - Customer selector: a dropdown populated from distinct context_tag values already present in Postgres (SELECT DISTINCT cmetadata->>'context_tag' FROM langchain_pg_embedding) — this is the one place the app reads back what's already been ingested, so the list is always accurate and there's no second place customer names are typed/could drift from what's actually indexed.
 - st.chat_input/st.chat_message loop for the question.
 - On each question: take the selected context_tag directly (no re-typing/re-slugifying needed, it's already stored), call the same retrieval logic as api.py's /query handler by importing vector_store.create_vector_store() directly (same direct-call reasoning as ingestion — one codebase, no network hop needed).
 - Render the LLM's answer as a chat bubble; render sources (list of filenames from QueryResponse/the raw metadata) underneath as a "Sources" caption — this is what lets a non-technical consultant trust and verify the answer.
 - Streamlit chosen over a custom React/HTML frontend: zero frontend build step, streamlit run app.py, looks like a real chat app via st.chat_message, and every line is plain Python that's trivial to walk through in an interview. Two tabs (st.tabs(["Ingest", "Ask"])) keep it a single app a consultant just opens — no separate ingestion tool to install or run.

