 Interview Talking Points (design decisions to be ready to explain)

 1. context_tag as the sole customer-scoping mechanism — one indexed JSONB metadata field, filtered on at query time. Chosen because customer isolation only needs to be logical, not physical (no per-customer tables/collections).
 2. Direct Python calls instead of HTTP hops within the same process, both for ingestion→RAG and chat→RAG. HTTP buys language/process decoupling; not needed when it's all one codebase — api.py remains available if the RAG store ever needs to be a separately-owned service.
 3. No LLM-driven relevance filtering at ingestion — ingest everything, let vector similarity + min_relevance do relevance filtering once, at query time. Keeps ingestion deterministic and debuggable.
 4. Agent scope is deliberately narrow — the LLM/tool-calling agent only formulates the web search query and optionally synthesizes a summary; file reading and the write path are plain deterministic Python. Avoids unexplainable "magic" while still legitimately using create_tool_calling_agent/AgentExecutor.
 5. Meeting summaries are just files in a folder, no calendar/notes-tool integration; email is out of scope entirely — building real OAuth integrations would burn the whole 2-day budget on plumbing unrelated to the core RAG/agent skill being demonstrated.
 6. Streamlit for the UI, single app with two tabs — satisfies the assignment's "non-technical person can just open and use it" requirement with a fraction of the build time of a custom frontend; one app means no separate ingestion tool to install or run.
 7. Customer name is always explicit user input, never inferred from folder/project names. A folder path is chosen per-run and typically named after the project, not the customer; guessing customer identity from it risks silently mis-scoping data (leaking one customer's info into another's answers). Both the folder path and the typed name persist across runs so this costs the user nothing after the first time.

